# Security Guidelines for EcoDrug

## Authentication & Authorization

### JWT Tokens
- Generate JWT tokens on successful login
- Set appropriate expiration time (7 days default)
- Store JWT_SECRET in environment variables, never in code
- Verify tokens on every protected route
- Include user ID and role in JWT payload

### Password Security
- **Always** hash passwords with bcrypt before storing
- Use salt rounds of at least 10
- Never log or expose passwords
- Implement password strength requirements (min 6 characters, consider complexity)
- Never return password field in API responses

### Protected Routes
- Apply authentication middleware to all protected routes
- Verify JWT token exists and is valid
- Check user permissions/roles where needed
- Return 401 for unauthenticated requests
- Return 403 for unauthorized access

## Input Validation & Sanitization

### Backend Validation
- Validate all user inputs on the server side
- Use express-validator for request validation
- Sanitize inputs to prevent injection attacks
- Validate email format, required fields, data types
- Never trust client-side validation alone

### Frontend Validation
- Implement client-side validation for better UX
- Validate email format, required fields, password match
- Show clear validation messages
- Remember: backend validation is the source of truth

## Data Protection

### Sensitive Data
- Store all secrets in `.env` file
- Never commit `.env` to version control
- Add `.env` to `.gitignore`
- Provide `.env.example` with required variables (without actual values)
- Use environment variables for: JWT_SECRET, MONGO_URI, API keys

### Database Security
- Use MongoDB authentication
- Implement proper access control
- Don't expose database connection strings
- Use MongoDB Atlas for production with IP whitelisting
- Regular backups

## CORS Configuration
- Configure CORS to allow only trusted origins
- In development: allow localhost
- In production: allow only your frontend domain
- Don't use wildcard (*) in production
- Set appropriate CORS headers (credentials, methods, headers)

## API Security Best Practices
- Implement rate limiting on authentication endpoints
- Use HTTPS in production
- Validate Content-Type headers
- Implement request size limits
- Log suspicious activities
- Return generic error messages (don't expose system details)

## User Data Privacy
- Only collect necessary user data
- Don't expose other users' private information
- Implement proper authorization checks
- Users should only access their own data
- Admin/moderator roles for accessing other users' data

## Content Moderation
- All user-generated content must be moderated before publishing
- Store moderation status in database
- Only admins can approve content
- Implement reporting functionality
- Filter inappropriate content

## Security Checklist
- [ ] Passwords hashed with bcrypt
- [ ] JWT tokens properly verified
- [ ] Environment variables for secrets
- [ ] Input validation on backend
- [ ] CORS properly configured
- [ ] MongoDB connection secured
- [ ] No sensitive data in logs
- [ ] Role-based access control implemented
- [ ] Rate limiting on auth endpoints
- [ ] Error messages don't expose system details
